---

<Layout title="About — Amy Young">

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ab-hero">
        <header class="page-head">
          <div class="sh-label rv">About</div>
          <h1 class="page-title rv">Hi, I'm<br><strong>Amy Young.</strong></h1>
          <div class="ab-leads">
            <p class="page-lead rv">I work at the intersection of data, product, and communications. Information specialist by title at the National Alzheimer's Coordinating Center, and in practice the person in the data, running funding program management, writing and building the web for the research community, and making the tools that speed all of it up.</p>
            <p class="page-lead rv">One thread runs through all of it: curiosity and problem solving. Understanding how things work, why people do what they do, and what becomes possible when you ask the right questions.</p>
            <p class="page-lead rv">AI agents now tie those threads together. Claude Code is my daily driver, and it lets me take on work I used to coordinate teams to do myself: research, design, build, ship. Underneath it is a model-agnostic system I designed and self-host, including a knowledge graph that turns raw research into something I can query. On top of that: 40+ custom skills and multi-agent routing across my research, content, and ops.</p>
            <p class="page-lead rv">It rewards exactly the kind of curious, cross-disciplinary thinking I've practiced my whole life.</p>
          </div>
        </header>

        <div class="ab-rail rv">
          <img class="ab-portrait" src="/assets/images/about/amy-young-about.jpg" alt="Amy Young" width="800" height="800">
          <div class="ab-focus">
            <span>AI &amp; Agentic Systems</span>
            <span>Product Strategy</span>
            <span>Research &amp; Analytics</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ab-where">Based in<br>Seattle, WA</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sec-rule"></div>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="ab-story">
        <div>
          <div class="sh-label rv">My story</div>
          <h2 class="ab-dh rv">Curiosity is<br><strong>the constant.</strong></h2>
        </div>

        <div class="ab-story-body rv">
          <p>Understanding how things work, why people do what they do, and what becomes possible when you ask the right questions.</p>

          <div class="ab-more" id="story-more" hidden>
            <p>My dad posed math problems on long car rides, not to test me but to teach me critical thinking: that if you understand a problem deeply enough, you'll find your way to a solution. Military service reinforced it differently, trading puzzles for the kind of resilience you only build by pushing past what feels like your limit.</p>
            <p>Sociology and Statistics gave me a lens I've never stopped using. People plus data, together. That combination led into market research, cross-functional leadership, and eventually product management, where I could combine analytical thinking with user empathy and strategic vision, across technology, healthcare, education, and beyond. Working across industries gave me a habit of finding unexpected connections, and an appreciation for how much context shapes the way we see problems.</p>
            <p>My work at the National Alzheimer's Coordinating Center brought it all into focus. Supporting the Alzheimer's research community showed me what's at stake when the right people, data, and tools find each other, and what it costs when they don't.</p>
            <p>I went back to UW for a Master of Science in Information Management. I expected it to sharpen the analytics and product side of the work. AI is what I found there, and it has had my full attention ever since.</p>
            <p>Now, with AI, the work I used to coordinate teams to do, research, design, build, and ship, I'm increasingly doing myself. I spend a lot of time exploring what's possible: building apps, agents, and automated workflows, designing systems that actually work for the people using them.</p>
            <p class="ab-kicker">What an extraordinary time to be building.</p>
          </div>

          <button class="ab-toggle" id="story-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="story-more">Read more</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sec-rule"></div>

  <section class="sec sec-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sh">
        <div class="sh-label rv">Systems</div>
        <h2 class="sh-title rv">The systems I build and run.</h2>
        <p class="sh-desc rv">Every one of these is in daily use, running my own work rather than sitting in a demo.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sys-grid">
        {SYSTEMS.map((s) => (
          <div class="sys-card rv">
            <div class="sys-n">{s.n}</div>
            <div class="sys-stat">{s.stat}</div>
            <div class="sys-stat-l">{s.statLabel}</div>
            <h3 class="sys-t">{s.title}</h3>
            <p class="sys-d">{s.body}</p>
            <div class="sys-chips">
              {s.chips.map((c) => <span class="tag tag-accent">{c}</span>)}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sec-rule"></div>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="tk-layout">
        <div class="tk-left">
          <div class="sh-label rv">Toolkit</div>
          <h2 class="ab-dh rv">What I<br><strong>actually work in.</strong></h2>
          <p class="tk-desc rv">Six areas, all of them hands-on. Open any one for the specifics.</p>
          <button class="ab-toggle tk-all rv" id="tk-all" type="button">Expand all</button>
        </div>

        <div class="tk-list rv" id="tk-list">
          {TOOLKIT.map((t, i) => (
            <div class={`tk-item${i === 0 ? " open" : ""}`}>
              <button
                class="tk-btn"
                type="button"
                aria-expanded={i === 0 ? "true" : "false"}
                aria-controls={`tk-panel-${i}`}
              >
                <span class="tk-n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                <span class="tk-t">{t.title}</span>
                <span class="tk-sign" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
              </button>

              <div class="tk-panel" id={`tk-panel-${i}`} hidden={i !== 0}>
                <p class="tk-lede">{t.lede}</p>
                <ul class="tk-points">
                  {t.points.map((p) => (
                    <li class="tk-point"><span><strong>{p.k}:</strong> {p.v}</span></li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
                <div class="tk-chips">
                  {t.chips.map((c) => <span class="tag tag-accent">{c}</span>)}
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sec-rule"></div>

  <section class="sec sec-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sh">
        <div class="sh-label rv">Background</div>
        <h2 class="sh-title rv">Education, awards, and community.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-layout">
        <figure class="bg-figure rv">
          <img
            src="/assets/images/about/amy-young-msim-commencement.jpg"
            alt="Amy Young at her University of Washington MSIM commencement"
            width="900" height="1455" loading="lazy">
          <figcaption class="bg-cap">MSIM commencement, University of Washington Information School, 2024.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div>
          {BACKGROUND.map((e) => (
            <div class="award rv">
              <div>
                <div class="award-name">{e.name}</div>
                <div class="award-org">{e.org}</div>
              </div>
              <span class="award-yr">{e.yr}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sec-rule"></div>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sh">
        <div class="sh-label rv">Experience</div>
        <h2 class="sh-title rv">How I got here.</h2>
        <p class="sh-desc rv">Four stages: research, ownership, agency, and the work I do now.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cj-list">
        {CAREER.map((r) => (
          <div class="cj-item rv">
            <div class="cj-yr">{r.yr}</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="cj-role">{r.role}</h3>
              <div class="cj-org">{r.org}</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>

<script>
  // My Story expander. Astro types HTMLElement.hidden as string | boolean, so the read
  // side is coerced with !! — the same pattern the layout's contact panel uses.
  const storyToggle = document.getElementById("story-toggle");
  const storyMore = document.getElementById("story-more");

  if (storyToggle && storyMore) {
    storyToggle.addEventListener("click", () => {
      const open = !!storyMore.hidden;
      storyMore.hidden = !open;
      storyToggle.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(open));
      storyToggle.textContent = open ? "Read less" : "Read more";
    });
  }

  // Toolkit accordion. Multiple panels may be open at once (unlike the homepage's
  // .hero-svc rail, which is one-at-a-time) because these are reference lists a reader
  // compares side by side, not a hero teaser.
  const tkList = document.getElementById("tk-list");
  const tkAll = document.getElementById("tk-all");

  if (tkList) {
    const items = Array.from(tkList.querySelectorAll(".tk-item"));

    const setItem = (item: Element, open: boolean) => {
      const btn = item.querySelector(".tk-btn");
      const panel = item.querySelector(".tk-panel");
      if (!(panel instanceof HTMLElement)) return;
      item.classList.toggle("open", open);
      panel.hidden = !open;
      btn?.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(open));
    };

    const syncAllLabel = () => {
      if (!tkAll) return;
      const allOpen = items.every((i) => i.classList.contains("open"));
      tkAll.textContent = allOpen ? "Collapse all" : "Expand all";
    };

    items.forEach((item) => {
      const btn = item.querySelector(".tk-btn");
      btn?.addEventListener("click", () => {
        setItem(item, !item.classList.contains("open"));
        syncAllLabel();
      });
    });

    tkAll?.addEventListener("click", () => {
      const allOpen = items.every((i) => i.classList.contains("open"));
      items.forEach((item) => setItem(item, !allOpen));
      syncAllLabel();
    });

    syncAllLabel();
  }
</script>
